# Extending the π‐Conjugated System in Spiro‐Type Hole Transport Material Enhances the Efficiency and Stability of Perovskite Solar Modules

## abstract
Hole transport materials (HTMs) are a key component of perovskite solar cells (PSCs). The small molecular 2,2,7,7-tetrakis(N,N-di-p-methoxyphenyl)-amine-9,9-spirobifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD, termed Spiro) is the most successful HTM used in PSCs, but its versatility is imperfect. To improve its performance, we developed a novel spiro-type HTM (termed DP) by substituting four anisole units on Spiro with 4-methoxybiphenyl moieties. By extending the -conjugation of Spiro in this way, the HOMO level of the HTM matches well with the perovskite valence band, enhancing hole mobility and increasing the glass transition temperature. DP-based PSC achieves high power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) of 25.24 % for small-area (0.06 cm2 ) devices and 21.86 % for modules (designated area of 27.56 cm2 ), along with the certified efficiency of 21.78 % on a designated area of 27.86 cm2 . The encapsulated DP-based devices maintain 95.1 % of the initial performance under ISOS-L-1 conditions after 2560 hours and 87 % at the ISOS-L-3 conditions over 600 hours.

Extending the $\pmb{\pi}$ -Conjugated System in Spiro-Type Hole Transport Material Enhances the Efficiency and Stability of Perovskite Solar Modules
## Introduction
In the last decade, the performance of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has improved considerably with respect to their power conversion efficiency (PCE), which has reached $26.0\%^{[1]}$ and their operation stability. However, transferring these achievements from individual small-size devices to large-area modules remains challenging.[2] The short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ and open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ are mainly determined by the intrinsic properties of the perovskite materials,[3] and both of these parameters increase in a limited range with seemingly few options for further improvement, leaving more flexibility for improving the fill factor (FF).[4]
Improvements in the FF can be achieved by reducing non-radiative recombination loss, as well as accelerating charge transfer and extraction.[5] Significant efforts have been made to obtain high-quality perovskite films with few trap densities, including increasing the grain size, reducing the grain boundaries, changing crystal orientation and dimensions, and passivating defects in perovskite films.[6] Beyond that, the structure of the hole transporting material (HTMs) influences the FF, with the goal of increasing it to the Shockley-Quisser (S-Q) limit.[6c,7] The best performing devices use $^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}$ -tetrakis(N,N-di-p-methoxyphenyl)- amine- $^{.9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirobifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD, termed “Spiro”) as hole transporting layers (HTLs) as its energy levels match those of perovskite films.[8] However, Spiro is limited by low conductivity and hole mobility, reducing its overall performance.[7a,9] Therefore, alternative HTMs with superior carrier transporting and extraction properties should improve the photovoltaic performance and stability of PSCs.
In the molecular design rule for HTM, the extending π- conjugation is expected to simultaneously improve various properties of HTM, such as higher glass transition temperature $(T_{\mathrm{g}})$ due to increasing molecular weight and tight packing, lower highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) level due to broad distribution of the electron cloud, and above two points are also the reasons for high hole mobility.[10] Herein, we describe a novel spiro-type HTM (“termed DP”, Figure 1a) in which the partial anisole groups on the “spiro” part of Spiro have been substituted by 4- methoxybiphenyl units. This change increases the $T_{\mathrm{g}}$ , and the extended $\pi$ system lowers the HOMO level, combining to improve the stability, conductivity and hole mobility.
Moreover, DP is soluble in chlorobenzene, which favors miscibility with dopants to afford HTLs with uniform morphologies. The DP-based small-area devices deliver the highest efficiency of $25.24\%$ , and the corresponding perovskite solar modules (PSMs) achieve the certified efficiency of $21.78\%$ on a designated area of $27.86\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ .
## Results and Discussion
DP-based small-area devices were constructed with a planar n-i-p configuration consisting of FTO/compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ( $\approx10\:\mathrm{nm}$ )/compact $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{\Pi}\approx10\mathrm{nm})$ /perovskite $(800\mathrm{nm})/$ OAI $(\approx20\:\mathrm{nm})/\mathrm{HTL}$ ( $220\mathrm{nm}$ for Spiro and $170\mathrm{nm}$ for DP)/ Au $(70\mathrm{nm})$ , where FTO is fluorine-doped tin oxide, and OAI is octyl ammonium iodide (Figure 2a). The optimized thickness of DP is preliminary obtained by varying the deposition concentration, and the detailed data is summarized in Table S2. Figure 2b shows the current density-voltage $(J{-}V)$ curves and corresponding photovoltaic parameters of the best devices using optimized deposition concentration. The Spiro-based device yields an overall efficiency of $24.34\%$ with a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $25.97\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $1.131\mathrm{V}$ and an FF of $82.9\%$ , whereas the DP-based device delivers a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $26.13\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $1.138\mathrm{V}$ and an FF of $84.9\%$ , ultimately yielding a high efficiency of $25.24\%$ . The increased FF may be ascribed to the high charge carrier mobility and high charge extraction ability of the DP-based devices (see below).[13a,15] The slightly higher $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of the DPbased devices is presumably due to the lower HOMO level compared to Spiro, and the slightly enhanced $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ could be due to the higher charge carrier mobility of the DP HTM over Spiro.[7a, 16]
The statistical distribution of both devices is consistent with the champion devices with minimal variation, and the average PCE of $24.86\%$ for the DP-based devices is higher than the PCE of $23.73\%$ for the Spiro-based devices (Figure S8). Accordingly, the current output at maximum power point (MPP) over 512 seconds demonstrates a steadystate efficiency of $24.80\%$ for the DP-based device (Figure 2c), higher than $24.02\%$ obtained for the Spiro-based device, demonstrating good operation stability. The integrated current densities from the external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of both devices match closely with those obtained from the $J{-}V$ curves, indicating the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ mismatch is negligible (Figure 2d).
When PSC devices are scaled up to large areas, the most significant efficiency losses are due to the increased series resistance caused by the restricted sheet conductivity of FTO and by the non-uniformity of the functional layer over large areas.[2b,17] The former loss can be partially overcome by connecting thin strips in series to afford PSMs, and the latter can be improved by using the vacuum-assisted gaspump method to fabricate high-quality large-scale perovskite films.[2a] A uniform HTM layer is required to ensure efficient carrier extraction from the perovskite layer.[12,18] Consequently, PSMs consisting of eight sub-cells connected in series with a geometrical fill factor (GFF) of $93.7\%$ were investigated. The current-voltage $(I{-}V)$ curves of the PSMs with a designated area of $27.56\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ were measured in a reverse scan with a PCE of $21.86\%$ achieved for the DPbased PSM, higher than the value of $20.58\%$ for the Spirobased PSM (Figure 2e), with a slight increase in $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and a dramatic improvement of the FF in the DP-based PSMs. The efficiency of the DP-based PSMs was certified by an independent accredited organization, the National PV Industry Measurement and Testing Center (NPVM) in Fuzhou, China. The certified efficiency is $21.78\%$ on a designated area of $27.86\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ (Figures 2f and S9). To the best of our knowledge, this value is the highest certified efficiency for a PSM with a designated-area exceeding $20\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ .
The statistical analysis of photovoltaic parameters based on 20 modules is presented in Figure S10. An average efficiency of $19.93\%$ and $21.24\%$ for the Spiro- and DPbased PSMs, respectively, showing good reproductivity, was achieved. These results confirm that the DP HTM layers facilitate efficient charge extraction and reduce charge resistive losses.
To recognize the origins of the enhanced photovoltaic performance, the morphological and electrical characteristics of the HTLs deposited on the perovskite layers were investigated. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images (Figure S11a and S11b) show that both the Spiro and DP layers are smooth and pinhole-less. As shown in the atomic force microscopy (AFM) images (Figures S11c and S11d), the DP film has a smooth surface with a higher uniformity and a lower root-mean-square (RMS) of ${\approx}1.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ compared to the Spiro film with a RMS of ${\approx}4.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Accordingly, the conductive atomic force microscopy (c-AFM) measurements (Figure S12) exhibit an average conductivity $(\sigma_{0})$ of $300\mathrm{pA}$ for the Spiro film that increases to $400\ \mathrm{pA}$ for the DP film. The $\sigma_{0}$ value can also be determined by measuring the $J_{-}V$ curve of the device with a FTO/HTL/Au structure (Figure 3a), using the equation $\sigma_{0}=J d/V$ , where $d$ is the thickness of HTM layer, affording $\sigma_{0}$ values of $1.23\times10^{-4}$ and $5.13\times10^{-4}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ for the Spiro and DP films, respectively. The $\sigma_{0}$ value of the DP layer is consistent with the extended $\pi$ -conjugation in the compound that improves the charge carrier mobility of the HTL.[8b,12] Hole mobility of the HTL can be calculated from the space-charge limited current (SCLC) with a device structure comprising FTO/PEDOT:PSS/HTL/Au (Figure 3b), where PEDOT:PSS corresponds to poly(3,4-ethylene-dioxythiophene): poly(styrenesulfonic acid). The corresponding hole mobilities of the Spiro and DP HTLs were estimated to be $2.48\times10^{-3}$ and $5.19\times10^{-3}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{2}\thinspace\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , respectively, further validating facilitated charge carrier transfer in the DP HTL.
Charge transfer dynamics at the perovskite/HTL interface were investigated by femtosecond transient adsorption spectroscopy (fs-TAS).[19] As shown in Figure S13, the main photobleaching (PB) peak located at around $805\mathrm{nm}$ weakens with time. The dynamics of transient photoinduced bleaching and the corresponding decay kinetics were thoroughly investigated by tri-exponential fitting analysis (Figure 3c) and are summarized in Table S3. The fast lifetime $(\tau_{1})$ is related to charge carrier trapping at the perovskite/ HTL interface and grain boundaries.[20] Compared to the $\tau_{1}$ value of 3.8 ps for the Spiro HTL, the shorter $\tau_{1}$ of 1.9 ps for the DP HTL indicates quicker trap filling, leading to larger splitting in the quasi-Fermi energy levels, which explains the slight enhancement in $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ in the DP-based device. Moreover, free-charge carriers are injected more quickly into the DP layer, improving the FF and PCE. The second lifetime $\left(\tau_{2}\right)$ may be attributed to hole extraction from the perovskite into the HTL.[20,21] The $\tau_{2}$ value shortens from 197.5 to 77.2 ps when the Spiro HTL is substituted with DP. The faster $\tau_{2}$ indicates faster hole extraction. Additionally, the long lifetime $\left(\tau_{3}\right)$ may be assigned to the exciton recombination time. Similar to $\tau_{1}$ and $\tau_{2}$ , the $\tau_{3}$ value was also found to be shorter for the DP HTL, demonstrating that the exciton can be quickly collected followed by slow back-electron transfer by the DP layer.[22] Time-resolved photoluminescence (PL) measurements were used to elucidate the dynamics of the charge extraction process (Figure 3d). Compared to Spiro HTL $\scriptstyle\tau_{1}=13.9$ ns and $\tau_{2}=66.6$ ns), DP HTL has a shorter lifetime $_{\tau_{1}=12.4}$ ns and $\tau_{2}=57.6$ ns), suggesting that holes can be efficiently extracted from the perovskite layer to the DP layer.[19] This finding is in agreement with the TAS decay study, further confirming accelerated charge extraction in the DP-based devices.
To gain deeper insight into the underlying mechanism for the improvement in the FF by the DP layer, contributions of non-radiative recombination and charge resistive loss were compared (Table S4). The theoretical FF in the absence of charge-transport losses $(\mathrm{FF}_{0})$ can be calculated by coupling with light intensity-dependent $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ values (ideality factor, $n_{\mathrm{id,}}^{\mathrm{}}$ ) using the following equations:[2a,4a]
$$
\begin{array}{l}{\displaystyle\mathrm{FF}_{\mathrm{0}}=\frac{\nu_{\mathrm{oc}}-\ln(V_{\mathrm{oc}}+0.72)}{\nu_{\mathrm{oc}}+1}}\\ {\displaystyle V_{\mathrm{oc}}=\frac{n_{\mathrm{id}}k_{\mathrm{B}}T}{e}\mathrm{ln}\left(\frac{J_{\mathrm{sc}}}{J_{0}}\right)}\end{array}
$$
where $\nu_{\mathrm{oc}}$ is the $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ normalized by the thermal energy $(\mathrm{n_{id}}k_{\mathrm{B}}T/e)$ , $k_{\mathrm{B}}$ is the Boltzmann constant, $T$ is the absolute temperature, $e$ is the elementary charge, and $J_{0}$ is the saturation current density at reverse bias. By fitting the $J{-}V$ curves based on a model heterojunction solar cell, as shown in Figures S14a and S14b, both $n_{\mathrm{id}}$ (1.31) and $J_{\mathrm{o}}$ $(5.1\times$ $10^{-11}\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2})$ of the DP-based device are estimated to be much lower than those of the Spiro-based device (1.48 and $3.1\times10^{-9}\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2},$ , respectively, suggesting the DP-based device reduces recombination leakage compared to the Spiro-based device. By analyzing the FF loss between the measured values and the S–Q limit (Figure S14c), the DPbased device shows a clearly mitigated charge transport loss and a slight non-radiative loss, which might be attributed to the reduced charge transport resistance of the more uniform DP film (Figure S14d).[4a] Series resistance $(R_{\mathrm{s}})$ in the DPbased device is $0.12\Omega\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , lower than that of $0.19\Omega\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ recorded for the Spiro-based device, further confirming fast charge transport and hence the enhancement in the FF and PCE. The potential improvement in FF in terms of hole mobility and thickness of the HTL layer was simulated based on the finite-element method (Table S5).[2a,23] As shown in Figure 3e, the improved FF in the DP-based device may be ascribed to the slightly decreased thickness of the HTL and the high hole mobility. In addition, the FF in the PSC devices is primarily dependent on the hole mobility, and thus, could be enhanced by up to $88\%$ with a corresponding PCE of $27\%$ if the HTL used has a high hole mobility of $0.01\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathbf{V}^{-1}\mathbf{s}^{-1}$ (Figure 3f).
To verify the intrinsic stability of the DP HTM, differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) of DP was performed and Figure S15 shows a glass transition temperature $(T_{\mathrm{g}})$ of $149^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (second heating scan), higher than that of the Spiro $(122^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ,[11b] indicating that DP HTM should be thermally stable in devices in the presence of dopants.[11a] Long-term operational stability was then monitored under maximum power point tracking under ambient conditions under one sun LED illumination. As shown in Figure 4a, the encapsulated Spiro-based devices lose $19.0\%$ of normalized PCE after aging 2560 hours, whereas the DP devices retain $95.1\%$ of their initial efficiency. The desirable charge transport properties of DP facilitate efficient charge carrier extraction, which favors decreased average carrier concentration in the DP-based devices, effectively restricting phase separation of the perovskite components.[24] Encouragingly, the DP-based devices also show excellent stability at $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $85\%$ RH (ISOS-L-3 protocol) and surpass the Spirobased devices $(66.5\%)$ , retaining $87\%$ of the initial performance for over 600 hours of continuous illumination, which is ascribed to the high $T_{\mathrm{g}}$ of DP (Figure 4b).[8b,11a]
## Conclusion
A new spiro-type HTM was prepared with an extended π- conjugated system that enhances the conductivity and hole mobility of the HTL. The high quality of the DP film and well-matched energy-level alignment ensures high charge extraction and transport in the resulting PSC devices. Accordingly, DP-based perovskite solar modules achieve the highest certified PCE of $21.78\%$ on a designated area of $27.86\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . Notably, significant improvements in long-term stability under ISOS-L-1 and ISOS-L-3 conditions were achieved in DP-based devices, which retain $95\%$ and $87\%$ of their initial performance, respectively, being more stable than the Spiro-based devices. These results demonstrated this new spiro-type HTM is ideal for high efficiency and stable PSCs and highlight the importance of developing high-performance of HTMs via the introduction of extended $\pi$ -conjugated systems, for efficient, stable, and scalable perovskite solar cells.
